  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Find the antiderivative of the function by Substitution</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Find the antiderivative of the function \(\displaystyle\int{@f_x@}dx\) by substitution</span><strong></strong>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Worked out solution</p>
<p> Recall that inorder to find the integral of a function using u-substitution, the function needs to be in the form \(\displaystyle\int\) \(f(g(x))g'(x)dx\), where \(g'(x)\) is the derivative of \(g(x)\)</p>

<p>From here we can easily carry out \(u\)-substitution and then integrate \(f(u)\) using the power rule. So, </p>
<p> Let \(u\)= {@var@}, \(du = {@diff_var@}, dx=\frac{-du}{@aa*xx@}\)</p>
<p>Substituting for u we get:</p>
<p> \(\displaystyle\int{@f_x@}dx\)= \(\displaystyle\int {@xx@}(u^{@1/power@}) \frac{-du}{@aa*xx@}\) </p>
<p>= \(\displaystyle\int (u^{@1/power@}) \frac{-{@xx@}du}{@aa*xx@}\) </p>
<p>= \(\displaystyle\int (u^{@1/power@}) \frac{-du}{@aa@}\) </p>
<p>= \( \frac{-1}{@aa@} \displaystyle\int (u^{@1/power@}) du \) </p>
<p>= \( \frac{-1}{@aa@}[{@int_u@}+c]\) </p> <br>
<p> But \(u\)= {@var@}, therefore we need to plug u back into the equation and simplify to get:</p>
<p>\( \frac{-1}{@aa@}[{@int_u@}+c]\)={@ta_1@} </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
